`^5.0.0`, because version 6 peer-requires Vite `^8` and imports `vite/internal`,
a subpath Vite 7 does not export. If `wxt prepare` ever crashes on that subpath,
run `npm ls @vitejs/plugin-react`, then `npm install` to re-apply the override.
**tsconfig:** `extension/tsconfig.json` extends `./.wxt/tsconfig.json`, which
WXT regenerates on `wxt prepare` and `.gitignore` excludes. It must set
`"jsx": "react-jsx"` itself. `@wxt-dev/module-react` only configures Vite and
never writes that option, so without it `npm run build` passes and
`npm run compile` fails on every `.tsx` file. The generated base also sets
`noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true`, which makes every array index read
`T | undefined`; `src/db/cards.test.ts` handles that in one `onlyCard()` helper. It also sets
`noImplicitOverride: true`, which is why every member of `ErrorBoundary` that
React's `Component` already declares carries an `override` modifier. Its `lib`
is ESNext plus the DOM libs and it pulls in no Node types, so any test that
imports a Node builtin needs `@types/node`, now a dev dependency.
`src/substack/extract.test.ts` reads `node:fs` and is the only such file.
`npm test` will not catch a missing type; Vite strips types without reading
them. `npm run compile` is what catches it.
