Milestone 3 is three items, not four: the parser, a manual Sync Saved, and the
first-run backlog import. The explicit unsave is cut.
**Why:** Four decisions, each put to the developer and each with a rejected
alternative worth keeping.

**Milestone 3 is read-only against Substack.** The explicit unsave would be the
first irreversible outward action the extension takes, and `spike/README.md`
records it as the most fragile thing on the site: the control does not exist in
the DOM until a Radix popover mounts, its classes are hashed and regenerated per
mount, and its label is inverted, reading `Save` when the article is NOT saved.

**Saved is an inbox to drain, not a mirror to reconcile.** The board owns
status; sync only pulls in. An article in Processed is done and sync must never
resurrect it, even while it is still saved on Substack. The mirror model was
rejected because it makes an unsave tapped on a phone a destructive input to a
card holding notes and quotes.

**Sync fetches nothing over the network.** The first answer in the brainstorm
was to fetch each new article and run `extractArticleMeta` over it, because a
synced card would otherwise carry no reading estimate and the board's Max
minutes filter HIDES cards with no estimate - an 85-article backlog invisible to
the filter the plan names as a success criterion. Reading the fixture overturned
that: `.reader2-item-meta` holds `Author∙14 min read` on 58 of 60 entries, so
the list already yields publication, date, title, subtitle, author, and a
duration for free. One page load instead of 85 requests, and no partial-failure
class at all. Substack's estimate wins over `readingMinutes()` for synced cards.

**Podcasts and videos are imported and marked.** Two fixture entries read
`1 hr 6 min watch`. `Card` gains `medium?: 'read' | 'watch' | 'listen'`,
optional and unindexed, so the Dexie schema STAYS AT VERSION 2 and there is no
migration.

**The rule the whole design rests on:** the missing pass runs only on a run
believed complete. Zero entries parsed, or a scroll loop that stopped on its
cap, skips it entirely. Without that guard one renamed Substack class turns "I
could not read the page" into a `syncWarning` on every card on the board. Same
shape as 2B's write-then-increment: order the steps so a failure leaves a
recoverable state rather than a confident wrong one.

**Three things the existing code already does**, each read out of the source
before the spec claimed it. `Card` already carries `lastSeenInSaved`,
`syncWarning`, and `unsavedFromSubstack`, provisioned in Milestone 1 and never
written to, and `CardInput`'s doc comment already names Milestone 3's sync as a
producer. `mergeCard` spreads `...existing` and overwrites only four metadata
fields, so "never resurrect a Processed card" needs NO code and only a test.
`ingestCard`'s transaction joins an outer one of compatible scope, so
`applySync` can wrap the whole run.

**The parse is split across two files** because `substack/saved.ts` is injected
and may import nothing. The injected side returns raw strings; `parseItemMeta`
lives in `domain/` where Vitest can reach it. It must match the duration from
the END of the string rather than split on the `∙`, because one fixture entry
reads `Wyndo and Dheeraj Sharma∙ 1 hr 6 min watch` and an author name can carry
the separator.

**Files:** `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-31-milestone-3-design.md` (new),
`changes.log`.
**Verification:** Not run. No code changed. Every claim about the existing code
was read out of it: `mergeCard` in `domain/ingest.ts`, `ingestCard` and its
transaction in `db/cards.ts`, the sync fields on `Card` in `domain/types.ts`.
Every claim about the Saved list was measured against
`spike/fixtures/saved-list.html` with linkedom: 60 entries, 58 with
`.reader2-item-meta`, and the two `watch` items named.
