to name real files, and the plan corrects them rather than leaving the executor
to discover them.

`substack/selectors.ts` cannot exist. `scripting.executeScript` ships a function
into the page by calling `Function.prototype.toString()` on it, so the function
cannot close over an imported `SELECTORS` const. Such code compiles,
type-checks, and then throws a bare `ReferenceError` inside Substack's document.
The selectors live inside the injected function's own body, in
`substack/extract.ts`. The same constraint forces the `doc: Document = document`
default parameter, which is what keeps the extractor testable: injection calls
it with no arguments and the page's `document` resolves at runtime, while tests
pass a linkedom document straight in.

The publication host fallback moved from the extractor to the background.
`publicationFromHost` is `domain/` code, and `substack/` imports nothing.

Tasks 4 to 6 are reordered. The spec listed the click wiring before the panel it
opens, which would mean writing a throwaway panel and replacing it two tasks
later. The plan builds `CardEditor`, then `ReadingPanel`, then wires the click.

One runtime-only trap is recorded in the plan because no test catches it:
`sidePanel.open()` must be the first `await` in the click handler. Chrome ties
it to the user gesture, and the gesture is spent once an unrelated async
operation resolves.

Also confirmed against `node_modules`: WXT adds the `sidePanel` permission and
`side_panel.default_path` itself when it finds a sidepanel entrypoint, so
`wxt.config.ts` declares only `activeTab` and `scripting`.
**Files:** `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-29-milestone-2a.md`, `changes.log`
