### 2026-08-28 - Dedup cards on article identity, not on the URL string

**What changed:** Added `articleKey()` to `domain/url.ts` and an `articleKey`
field to `Card`. `ingestCard()` and `restoreCards()` now look up by that key
instead of by `url`. Dexie moved to version 2, which indexes the field and
backfills every existing row. `mergeCard()` no longer overwrites `url`.
**Why:** Substack serves one article from the publication's custom domain, its
`*.substack.com` subdomain, and the `open.substack.com/pub/...` share route.
`canonicalizeUrl` folds only the query, the hash, and a trailing slash, so two
routes to one article produced two cards. The unique `&url` index proved it:
identical strings could never have been stored, so the rows had to differ.
`articleKey` reads the publication from the host (or from `/pub/<name>/` on the
share route) and pairs it with the article slug. The rejected options were
resolving the true URL over the network, which needs broad host permissions and
breaks the local-first promise, and keying on the slug alone, which would merge
two publications that both use `/p/welcome`. The guess fails toward a duplicate
card, never toward a wrong merge.
**Files:** `extension/src/domain/url.ts`, `types.ts`, `card.ts`, `ingest.ts`,
`extension/src/db/schema.ts`, `cards.ts`, `extension/src/test-support/factory.ts`,
their tests, new `extension/src/db/schema.test.ts`, `docs/learning-notes.md`.
**Verification:** `npx vitest run` passes, 92 tests across 6 files.
`npm run build` succeeds. Not checked in the browser yet.
**State after:** New adds dedup across all three routes. A board that already
holds a duplicate pair keeps it; the version 2 upgrade backfills both rows and
leaves them. `articleKey` is not a unique index, on purpose.
**Next up:** Run `extension/MANUAL-CHECKS.md`, delete the leftover duplicate on
the board by hand, then commit.
**Open questions:** A custom domain unrelated to the publication's Substack name
still splits into two cards. Milestone 2's content script can read
`<link rel="canonical">` from the page DOM and settle it with no network call.

### 2026-08-28 - Repair the `@vitejs/plugin-react` drift that broke `wxt prepare`

**What changed:** Ran `npm install` in `extension/`. That re-resolved the tree
against the `overrides` block already in `package.json` and replaced the nested
`@wxt-dev/module-react/node_modules/@vitejs/plugin-react@6.1.0` with `5.2.0`. No
source file and no config file was edited. Ticked Task 6 Step 7 in the Milestone
1 plan.
**Why:** `npm run compile` reported about 200 type errors, all inside
`node_modules`, all of the form "Cannot find name `Set`" and "Cannot find global
value `Promise`". Those are the ES5 defaults, so `tsc` was running with no
config. The chain: the installed `@vitejs/plugin-react` was `6.1.0`, which
peer-requires Vite `^8` and imports `vite/internal`; this project pins Vite
`7.3.6`, which does not export that subpath; so `wxt prepare` crashed while
resolving user modules; so `.wxt/tsconfig.json` was never generated and stayed at
its `{}` placeholder; so `extends "./.wxt/tsconfig.json"` resolved to an empty
config. `npm ls @vitejs/plugin-react` already flagged the copy as `invalid`.
Editing `tsconfig.json` to spell out `lib`, `target`, and `moduleResolution` was
rejected: it would have hidden a broken install behind a duplicated config.
**Files:** `extension/package-lock.json` (regenerated),
`extension/node_modules/`, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-26-milestone-1.md`.
**Verification:** `npm run compile` exits 0. `npm run build` exits 0 and writes
`.output/chrome-mv3/`. `npm test` passes 47 tests across 4 files.
